==============================================================================
EXACT DECOMPOSITION OF A_4  (verifying a_4 -> 0, the cherry is unique)
==============================================================================
  p |          A_4 |   A_4/p^4 |        T_a |  T_a/p^3 |  T_b/p^3 | T_c=-(p-3)p(p-1)?
  7 |          336 |   0.13994 |       -168 |  -0.4898 |   0.0000 |               OK
 11 |         1760 |   0.12021 |       -880 |  -0.6612 |   0.0000 |               OK
 19 |        10944 |   0.08398 |      -5472 |  -0.7978 |   0.0000 |               OK
 23 |        20240 |   0.07233 |     -10120 |  -0.8318 |   0.0000 |               OK
 31 |        52080 |   0.05639 |     -26040 |  -0.8741 |   0.0000 |               OK
 43 |       144480 |   0.04226 |     -72240 |  -0.9086 |   0.0000 |               OK
 47 |       190256 |   0.03899 |     -95128 |  -0.9163 |   0.0000 |               OK
 59 |       383264 |   0.03163 |    -191632 |  -0.9331 |   0.0000 |               OK
 67 |       566016 |   0.02809 |    -283008 |  -0.9410 |   0.0000 |               OK

Interpretation: A_4/p^4 -> 0 (a_4 = 0); T_a/p^3, T_b/p^3 stay O(1) bounded,
confirming T_a,T_b = O(p^3) [Weil square-root cancellation], A_4 = O(p^3).
=> the ONLY cluster integral that survives is a_2 = 1 (the cherry).

==============================================================================
CONSEQUENCE: R(p) -> exp(a_2) = exp(1) = e.  Convergence rate:
==============================================================================
  p |      R(p) |      e-R | (e-R)*sqrt(p) |   (e-R)*p
  3 |   2.00000 |  0.71828 |        1.2441 |    2.1548
  7 |   2.40000 |  0.31828 |        0.8421 |    2.2280
 11 |   2.43951 |  0.27878 |        0.9246 |    3.0665
 19 |   2.52715 |  0.19113 |        0.8331 |    3.6315
 23 |   2.55698 |  0.16130 |        0.7736 |    3.7099

(e-R)*sqrt(p) ~ const => convergence is O(1/sqrt p): slow, which is exactly
why 5 data points (max p=23, R=2.557, still 6% below e) could not distinguish
e from a larger constant by extrapolation alone. The cluster expansion settles it.
